---

Why BUSL 1.1 (instead of MIT / Apache 2.0 / AGPL / Unlicense):

  - MIT / Apache 2.0 would let any competitor take this code, rebrand
    it, and run a competing SaaS with no obligation back. Not ideal
    for a revenue-generating platform.

  - AGPL would force every modification to be published under AGPL
    too, including proprietary forks. Strong but legally heavier and
    off-putting to enterprise buyers and acquirers.

  - BUSL 1.1 (used by HashiCorp, Sentry, MariaDB, CockroachDB) lets
    anyone read, fork, run for non-production purposes (development,
    testing, evaluation), but reserves commercial SaaS competition to
    the Licensor. After the Change Date the work converts to Apache 2.0
    and anyone can use it for anything.

Change Date of 2031-09-11 (5 years from 2026-09-11) chosen to give
the Licensor a defensible commercial window before the code becomes
fully open.
